## 1. Section I: Introduction & Motivation (New Draft Text)

### 1.1 The Heterogeneous 5G/6G Backhaul Landscape
Next-generation 5G-Advanced and emerging 6G architectures increasingly distribute compute workloads toward localized edge clouds, private enterprise networks, and tactical operational nodes[cite: 1, 2]. To ensure resilient connectivity to core cloud infrastructure and the public internet, these multi-homed edge nodes rely on concurrent, multi-backhaul paths spanning heterogeneous physical media[cite: 1, 2]. As standardized across recent 3GPP releases, a multi-access edge node may concurrently leverage:
1. High-capacity terrestrial fiber optics or fixed point-to-point millimeter-wave (mmWave) links offering sub-5 ms propagation delays[cite: 1, 2].
2. Public or private cellular macrocell wireless relays (e.g., 5G NR mid-band) subject to dynamic multi-user scheduling and intermediate buffering[cite: 1, 2].
3. Non-Terrestrial Networks (NTN), primarily Low Earth Orbit (LEO) mega-constellations (such as Starlink or OneWeb), which provide ubiquitous coverage across remote and underserved regions at the cost of higher base propagation delay (40–80 ms) and time-varying channel attenuation[cite: 1, 2].

### 1.2 The Steering Challenge & Divergent SLAs
The fundamental challenge in managing multi-backhaul edge architectures lies in the divergent Service Level Agreements (SLAs) demanded by co-located traffic flows[cite: 1, 2]. Ultra-Reliable Low-Latency Communication (URLLC) services—such as industrial closed-loop automation, remote telemetry, and tactical command—require deterministic, bounded round-trip times (RTT) where queuing delays or path degradation violate operational safety boundaries[cite: 1, 2]. Conversely, enhanced Mobile Broadband (eMBB) applications prioritize aggregate goodput over raw propagation delay[cite: 1, 2]. 

Static default routing fails to navigate this heterogeneity[cite: 1, 2]. When a primary link experiences channel degradation or sudden bufferbloat, flows bound to that interface suffer severe latency inflation or transport-layer collapse[cite: 1, 2]. While multi-path transport protocols such as MPTCP or QUIC connection migration have been proposed, they often retain damaged congestion window states upon path switching or require end-to-end support across client and server endpoints. A practical edge solution must dynamically monitor link quality and execute rapid, application-level flow re-anchoring across candidate backhauls while remaining fully compatible with standard single-path TCP stacks.

### 1.3 Methodological Gap & Proposed Architecture
Evaluating multi-backhaul steering algorithms under realistic transport-layer dynamics poses a significant experimental challenge[cite: 1, 2]. Live commercial field trials across 5G and satellite networks are non-reproducible, cost-prohibitive, and contaminated by uncalibrated background cross-traffic[cite: 1, 2]. Conversely, discrete-event simulators (e.g., ns-3) abstract away the host operating system's network stack[cite: 1, 2]. Crucial kernel phenomena—including Netlink interface notification latencies, TCP socket lifecycle states, route-cache metric pollution, and Linux socket buffer sizing—are omitted[cite: 1, 2].

To address this gap, this paper introduces a two-stage evaluation framework[cite: 1, 2]. In Stage 1, we deploy and calibrate a lightweight, kernel-native emulation platform based on Linux network namespaces (`netns`) and Traffic Control (`tc`), proving rate enforcement fidelity and demonstrating that application-level session re-anchoring introduces an invariant, payload-independent switching cost ($O(1)$ scaling)[cite: 1, 2]. In Stage 2, we evaluate a dynamic, latency-driven flow steering mechanism that actively detects in-path RTT degradation and re-anchors flows from impaired cellular or satellite links back to pristine low-latency channels without requiring protocol-level modifications[cite: 1, 2].

