## 3. Section V: Related Work & Discussion (New Draft Text)

### 5.1 Emulation Platforms vs. Simulators
Traditional performance evaluation of edge backhauling relies heavily on discrete-event simulators such as ns-3 or Omnet++. While simulators offer massive scalability, extensive studies have demonstrated significant divergence between simulated TCP congestion models and real Linux kernel implementations. In contrast, heavy physical testbeds utilizing SDR-based 5G gNodeBs or live LEO terminals lack deterministic repeatability and introduce confounding environmental noise. Containerized and namespace-based emulation platforms—such as Mininet—bridge this divide. Our work extends this paradigm by integrating non-intrusive Netlink telemetry, route-cache isolation, and multi-backhaul QoS pipelines to study transient transport dynamics during link migration.

### 5.2 3GPP AT3S vs. Application-Level Session Re-Anchoring
At the cellular architecture level, 3GPP Release 16 and Release 17 standardized Access Traffic Steering, Switching, and Splitting (AT3S), enabling User Equipment (UE) to steer traffic across 3GPP (cellular) and non-3GPP (Wi-Fi/satellite) paths. AT3S typically relies on Multi-Path TCP (MPTCP) or specialized IPsec multi-access tunnels. 

However, multi-path transport layers inherit significant performance degradation when migrating out of heavily lossy links. Under standard MPTCP or QUIC connection migration, unacknowledged packets and reduced congestion window states are often carried over or must undergo timeout-based recovery, stalling goodput. In contrast, our findings indicate that for bulk transfers undergoing severe channel degradation, executing an application-level session re-anchoring with state reset—incurring a small, deterministic $O(1)$ switching cost ($\Delta T \approx 55$ ms)[cite: 1, 2]—enables the transport engine to achieve rapid throughput recovery via exponential slow-start on the pristine backhaul.

