## 4. Section VI: Conclusion and Future Work (New Draft Text)

### 6.1 Conclusion
In this paper, we presented a kernel-native emulation platform and evaluation methodology for dynamic multi-backhaul management in next-generation 5G-Advanced and 6G edge networks[cite: 1, 2]. By combining Linux network namespaces, Traffic Control, and non-intrusive Netlink telemetry, our testbed replicates real-world kernel transport dynamics without physical hardware constraints[cite: 1, 2]. In Stage 1, empirical calibration proved the platform's rate-shaping accuracy, validated Mathis model goodput decay under loss, and established that session re-anchoring introduces an invariant $O(1)$ switching delay ($\Delta T \approx 55$ ms) regardless of file size[cite: 1, 2]. In Stage 2, we demonstrated a latency-driven selection and steering mechanism across heterogeneous Terrestrial, Cellular, and Satellite NTN profiles[cite: 1, 2]. Empirical results from POC-01 through POC-03 confirm that dynamic threshold-triggered re-anchoring successfully bounds round-trip times and restores pristine transmission efficiency when primary links undergo channel degradation[cite: 1, 2].

### 6.2 Future Work
While this work focused on single-objective latency steering, several critical research directions remain[cite: 1, 2]. We plan to extend this framework into an archival digital twin platform supporting[cite: 1, 2]:
1. **Multi-Criteria Decision-Making (MCDM)**: Upgrading link selection to formal utility optimization (e.g., TOPSIS or Analytic Hierarchy Process) balancing bandwidth, latency, and loss simultaneously[cite: 1, 2].
2. **Cost-Aware Hysteresis**: Utilizing our empirical handover penalty ($\Delta T \approx 55\text{ ms} + \text{RTT}$)[cite: 1, 2] to mathematically compute optimal anti-flapping thresholds under high link variance[cite: 1, 2].
3. **Non-Stationary NTN Dynamics**: Implementing time-varying satellite orbital pass-over traces, rain attenuation models, and Doppler shift variations[cite: 1, 2].
4. **Transport Inversion Dynamics**: Conducting an exhaustive multi-protocol comparative analysis between TCP CUBIC, TCP Reno, and BBR under dynamic path switching[cite: 1, 2].

